  "exercises": "Every refusal construction in the call closure of what the adapter can put in a cursor's methods[*].refusal (§3.2), read from the source: the four writes of counts[\"refusal\"] and the seven _Refused raises in analysis.py, and every Refusal and NoReferent that the relations.py functions those raises pass on can build, in their closure. Each construction's parts are constants, a choice of two constants, or a variable part with a bound: names, capabilities and REMEDIES text at the longest the registry gives (what is missing any subsequence of a schema's capabilities), the incomplete reason at its longest (§3.1), and every count at sys.maxsize, or the pair count n(n - 1)/2 of two such lengths. Each site's bytes take every part independently; its per_schema builds it from one schema's own parts, over the schemas its guard selects, as 3PO measured (6684968306). The largest is search_stub_resolution's refusal, 543 bytes, which has no variable part: REFUSAL_BYTES, a conservative bound, since the dispatch never builds it (reach: built when called directly, never through _stub_resolved_stream, whose line_status states come from the real Stream.supply). The largest the dispatch can build is largest_dispatched, 336 bytes. names lists every place analysis.py names a refusal, refused_uses every use of _Refused, and mutators every call in the closure that could copy a refusal with a field changed. The real _candidates, _full_shape and _stub_resolved_stream, run on streams whose every frame has a source label of 1, 4,096 and 65,536 bytes (and whose line_status is empty, blank or that label), build byte-identical refusals, all within REFUSAL_BYTES; _apply_declarations sets a frame's source only to \"declared\" or \"none\", and reads a declaration's source only to require one or to hand the beatgrid's to declare_beat, which reads none. A construction, variable part, name, use or call not listed here, a bound that moves, or a label that reaches a refusal fails the validator: REFUSAL_BYTES is then derived again.",
